   Inst Architects; Hong Kong Institut Engineers; Hong Kong Inst Surveyors;
   HKSTS; IUE; JSCE; RICS; Urban Planning Soc China; China Civil Engn Soc;
   Hong Kong Inst Util Specialists},
Abstract = {High density and compact city living is an inevitable future of human
   sustainable urbanisation in the coming era. High density and compact
   living saves on land resources, reduce travel energy costs, and provide
   a more multivalent, diverse and convenient living environment for its
   inhabitants. Governments and city planners around the world have been
   coining on increasing their city density. However, the associated urban
   climatic problems, like urban heat island, needs to be more carefully
   addressed when human living is closer together. Urban climatic studies
   have been a topical subject recently. Many cities around the world are
   now conducting detail studies to examine and deal with the problem. The
   experience of Hong Kong walking the path will be delineated. Since 1998,
   the Hong Kong Government of Hong Kong has conducted a number of studies,
   many of them have been promulgated as polices and practice notes for the
   local professionals to follow. Lately, there have been calls also to
   deal with the so call wall building problems, urban air ventilation and
   urban heat island issues of urban living in Hong Kong. The state of the
   art understanding will be disserted and the latest efforts, for example,
   the drafting of the Hong Kong Urban Climatic Map, will be elaborated.
   The paper aims to present a key but personal picture of efforts in Hong
   Kong in the last 10 or so years on urban climate for design and planning
   in Hong Kong.},
