   Algarve, PORTUGAL, APR 25-27, 2007},
Organization = {Wessex Inst Technol; Univ Thessaly; WIT Transact Built Environm; ASCE UK
   Int Grp},
Abstract = {In this paper, a method is developed to identify a typology of
   interrelated landscapes in the urban-rural realm, in order to describe
   the connection between landscape characteristics, natural environment
   characteristics and ecological strategies, finally translated into
   spatial planning styles. This interrelated complex of physical, social
   and managerial concepts may at first seem unusual, but the authors are
   convinced that this approach provides a useful insight. That one of the
   great challenges for planners today is to connect the best of two
   worlds: the one actually focussing on differentiation in governance
   style in the field of planning concepts, as well the one focussing the
   debate on landscape ecology on connectivity in spatial structures as the
   key towards sustainability in both nature development and sustainable
   urban planning.},
