Note = {4th International Congress on Clinical and Counselling Psychology
   (CPSYC), Kyrenia, CYPRUS, MAY 10-12, 2016},
Abstract = {The purpose of this study was to examine the extent to which cognitive
   emotion regulation components namely self-blame, other-blame,
   rumination, catastrophyzing, positive refocusing, positive reappraisal,
   planning and acceptance, influence psychological well-being among
   graduate students in one Malaysian university. A total of 534 graduate
   students, comprising of 155 males and 379 females, were selected using
   proportional sampling method among six faculties. Their mean age was 27
   years (SD = 4.7). Descriptive analysis showed that the influence of
   positive reappraisal was high amongst the respondents. While, the
   percentages were lower in other-blame and catastrophyzing. Using
   Structural Equation Modeling, the finding revealed that psychological
   well-being of graduate students influenced by planning, catastrophyzing,
   reappraisal, other-blame, self-blame, putting into perspective, and
   acceptance. In conclusion, seventh strategies mentioned above explained
   41\% of psychological well-being of graduates students. Beside this,
   based on the result the model fits the data. (C) 2016 Published by
   Future Academy www.FutureAcademy.org.uk},
