Title = {Nature-Based Solutions for Wastewater Treatment in the Hilly Areas of
   Mwanza City, Tanzania},
Booktitle = {WORLD ENVIRONMENTAL AND WATER RESOURCES CONGRESS 2025: COOL SOLUTIONS TO
   HOT TOPICS},
Year = {2025},
Pages = {1148-1165},
Note = {2025 World Environmental and Water Resources Congress, Anchorage, AK,
   MAY 18-21, 2025},
Abstract = {Sustainable wastewater management is a critical challenge for urban and
   rural areas worldwide. This challenge is particularly pronounced in
   hilly regions, where topographical constraints, limited accessibility,
   and resource scarcity often hinder the implementation of conventional
   wastewater treatment systems. Nature-based solutions (NBS) emerge as a
   sustainable and cost-effective approach in such contexts. These
   solutions leverage natural processes and ecosystems to treat and manage
   wastewater, ensuring environmental sustainability and public health
   protection. Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) 6 underscores the need
   for equitable sanitation, improved water quality, safety, and
   restoration of water-related ecosystems. Globally, over 80\% of
   wastewater is released untreated, posing significant public health and
   environmental risks. Approximately 2.4 billion people need improved
   sanitation, and 3.6 billion, half of the world's population, use
   sanitation services that leave human waste inadequately treated, causing
   1.4 million preventable deaths annually. In Tanzania, achieving SDG 6's
   sanitation target by 2030 requires sustainable wastewater treatment
   solutions to preserve ecosystems. In Mwanza City, only a tiny fraction
   of households are connected to centralized sewer systems, with 90\%
   relying on onsite sanitation like pit latrines and septic tanks. Poor
   sludge management contributes to waterborne diseases, and the city's
   hilly terrain complicates sanitation, leading to sewage overflows that
   contaminate Lake Victoria. This study aims to design nature-based
   solutions (NBS) for wastewater treatment in Mwanza's hilly areas,
   including Butimba, Igogo, Isamilo, Mkuyuni, Mkolani, Kirumba, Kitangiri,
   Nyakato, Nyamanoro, and Pasiansi. The research explores NBS
   technologies, such as waste stabilization ponds, constructed wetlands,
   and decentralized systems, to reduce pollution and protect Lake
   Victoria.},
