   GEOLOGY AND THE ENVIRONMENT, PROCEEDINGS, VOL 6},
Year = {2000},
Pages = {4327-4331},
Note = {8th International Congress of the
   International-Association-for-Engineering-Geology-and-the-Environment,
   VANCOUVER, CANADA, SEP 21-25, 1998},
Organization = {Int Assoc Engn Geol \& Environm},
Abstract = {Analysis of stable development of urban environment from ecological
   point of view revealed that the reaction of separate components of <<
   urban >> ecosystem (technobiogeosystem) to an external influence is
   subject to the general laws which can be described using Le Chatelier
   principle. According to this principle system stability is determined by
   its ability to response to an influence by involving compensation or
   counteraction processes to eliminate an influence consequences. <<
   Cornpensation >> approach to technobiogeosystem components stability
   assessment enables determination of limited maximum levels of an
   influence on a system and critical levels of its destruction determining
   system stability limits. It can serve as a methodological basis for
   environment monitoring, rational use of natural resources, and
   environment control.},
